<body class="marketing-page">
	<SiteNav />

	<main>

	<section class="hero alt-hero">
		<div class="hero-badge">Alternatives · Self-Hosted Email</div>
		<h1>The Microsoft 365 email alternative<br/><span>you actually own.</span></h1>
		<p class="alt-lead">
			Microsoft 365 keeps your mailboxes inside Microsoft's tenant, licensed per seat for
			as long as each person works there. Vectis Mail puts them on <em>your</em> server —
			full IMAP/SMTP mailboxes, your domains, DKIM/SPF/DMARC, webmail and a REST API, for a
			flat price instead of per-seat licensing. It's email infrastructure you own: it
			replaces the Exchange Online mailboxes in Microsoft 365, not the Office apps, Teams
			or SharePoint — that's the scope, and for most teams it's the part the bill is really for.
		</p>
		<div class="hero-buttons" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<a href="/getting-started/installation/" class="btn-primary">Install Vectis Mail free</a>
			<a href="/pricing/" class="btn-secondary">View pricing</a>
		</div>
		<div class="alt-meta" style="margin-top: 1.25rem;">
			<span>Last updated <time datetime={dateModified}>7 July 2026</time></span>
			<span>·</span>
			<span>By the Vectis Mail team</span>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 2rem;">
		<h2>At a glance</h2>
		<p class="subtitle">Email-to-email comparison: Vectis Mail vs the Exchange Online half of Microsoft 365. Current as of June 2026.</p>
		<div class="compare-table-wrap">
			<table class="compare-table">
				<thead>
					<tr><th></th><th>Vectis Mail</th><th>Microsoft 365 (Exchange Online)</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><th scope="row">What it is</th><td>Self-hosted email server (you run it)</td><td>Managed email inside Microsoft's cloud, part of a productivity suite</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Pricing model</th><td><strong>Flat per tenant</strong> — $0 Starter / $39 USD Pro per month</td><td>Per user, per month (scales with headcount)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Mailboxes</th><td><strong>Unlimited (Pro)</strong>; 25/domain on Starter</td><td>One licensed mailbox per paid user</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Domains</th><td>Up to 3 (Starter) · <strong>unlimited (Pro)</strong></td><td>Multiple domains per tenant</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Where mail lives</th><td><strong>Your server, your region, your jurisdiction</strong></td><td>Microsoft's tenant + datacentres</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Protocols</th><td>Standard IMAP / SMTP / POP3 + Roundcube webmail</td><td>Exchange / Outlook (IMAP/SMTP available, MAPI-native)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Mailbox storage</th><td>Bounded only by your disk</td><td>50 GB (Plan 1) / 100 GB (Plan 2) per mailbox</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">REST API for sending</th><td><strong>Yes</strong> — domain-scoped keys, batch, attachments</td><td>Graph API (suite-wide, not a flat-priced sending service)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Inbound webhooks</th><td><strong>Yes</strong> — HMAC-signed, retry-with-backoff</td><td>Via Graph subscriptions (more involved)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Spam filtering</th><td>Rspamd + optional ClamAV, your own rules</td><td>Exchange Online Protection (managed)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Office apps (Word/Excel/PPT)</th><td>No — email only</td><td>Yes (Standard/Premium)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Teams / SharePoint / OneDrive</th><td>No — email only</td><td>Yes</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Calendar / contacts</th><td>Not yet (Phase 4 roadmap)</td><td>Yes (full Exchange calendaring)</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Vendor compliance attestations</th><td>You are the data custodian</td><td>SOC 2 / ISO 27001 / HIPAA BAA</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">Lock-in</th><td>Standard protocols, data in your Postgres + filesystem</td><td>Microsoft tenant; migration is a project</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--sl-color-gray-3); margin-top: 0.5rem;">
			Microsoft 365 / Exchange Online pricing and storage retrieved 21 June 2026 from Microsoft. See sources below. Vectis Mail figures per <a href="/pricing/">/pricing</a>.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Why teams move to Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p class="subtitle">Where per-seat email licensing starts to hurt.</p>
		<div class="feature-card" style="margin-top: 1.5rem;">
			<ul style="margin-bottom: 0;">
				<li>Email is the part of Microsoft 365 you actually use, and you're paying per seat for mailboxes you could own outright.</li>
				<li>Your headcount is growing and the per-user bill grows with it — a flat $39/month stops the linear climb.</li>
				<li>Data sovereignty, residency, or jurisdiction matters: your mail belongs on a server you control, in a region you choose, not in Microsoft's tenant.</li>
				<li>You want to be immune to tenant-level outages and provider lock-in — standard IMAP/SMTP on your own domain, your data in your own Postgres.</li>
				<li>You're a developer or agency that wants a real sending API, inbound webhooks, and per-domain control over the mail stack.</li>
			</ul>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Own your mailboxes instead of licensing them</h2>
		<p>
			The framing Microsoft sells is a subscription: every employee is a "seat," and every
			seat is a recurring per-user charge for as long as they work there. Email — one of the
			oldest, most standardised technologies on the internet — gets billed like a SaaS product
			you rent forever.
		</p>
		<p>
			Vectis Mail inverts that. You run the server, so a mailbox is just a row in your own
			database and a maildir on your own disk. Adding the 10th mailbox or the 500th costs the
			same: nothing extra. Your mail lives where you deployed it — on a VPS in the region and
			jurisdiction you chose — not inside a Microsoft tenant subject to Microsoft's terms,
			Microsoft's jurisdiction, and Microsoft's tenant-wide outages. When a provider has a bad
			day, a tenant-level Exchange Online incident takes everyone down at once; a server you
			control has a blast radius of exactly your own organisation.
		</p>
		<p>
			And because it's all standard IMAP, SMTP, and POP3 on your own domain, there is no
			proprietary tenant to escape later. Migrating in is an IMAP sync; migrating out, if you
			ever wanted to, is the same. That's the difference between owning your mailboxes and
			licensing them.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Flat price vs per-seat licensing</h2>
		<p>
			Microsoft 365 and Exchange Online are priced per user per month, so the bill tracks your
			headcount. As of June 2026, Exchange Online Plan 1 is about <strong>$4/user/month</strong>
			(50 GB mailbox) and Plan 2 about <strong>$8/user/month</strong> (100 GB) on annual
			commitment. The fuller suites cost more, and Microsoft is <strong>raising prices from
			1 July 2026</strong>: Business Basic goes to $7/user/month and Business Standard to
			$14/user/month — increases Microsoft has tied to bundling Copilot Chat and added features.
		</p>
		<p>
			Vectis Mail is flat. Starter is $0 self-hosted (up to 3 domains, 25 mailboxes per domain);
			Pro is <strong>$39 USD per tenant per month</strong> for unlimited domains and unlimited
			mailboxes. Add a VPS to run it on and your fully-loaded cost is roughly $59/month whether
			you host 10 mailboxes or 500. Here's the same workforce, two billing models:
		</p>
		<div class="compare-table-wrap">
			<table class="compare-table">
				<thead>
					<tr><th>Mailboxes</th><th>Vectis Mail Pro (+ $20/mo VPS)</th><th>Exchange Online Plan 1 (~$4/user)</th><th>M365 Business Standard (~$14/user, Jul 2026)</th></tr>
				</thead>
				<tbody>
					<tr><th scope="row">10 users</th><td><strong>$59/mo flat</strong></td><td>~$40/mo</td><td>~$140/mo</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">50 users</th><td><strong>$59/mo flat</strong></td><td>~$200/mo</td><td>~$700/mo</td></tr>
					<tr><th scope="row">200 users</th><td><strong>$59/mo flat</strong></td><td>~$800/mo</td><td>~$2,800/mo</td></tr>
				</tbody>
			</table>
		</div>
		<p style="font-size: 0.85rem; color: var(--sl-color-gray-3); margin-top: 0.5rem;">
			Honest caveat: the Business Standard column buys the whole Office suite + Teams, not just
			email — it's not a like-for-like mailbox comparison. The Exchange Online Plan 1 column is
			the closest apples-to-apples (email only), and even there Vectis Mail Pro pulls ahead past
			roughly 15 mailboxes. Per-user figures are Microsoft's; see sources.
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Our scope: the mailbox half</h2>
		<p class="subtitle">One honest note on what Vectis Mail is — and isn't.</p>
		<div class="feature-card" style="margin-top: 0.5rem;">
			<p style="margin-bottom: 0;">
				Vectis Mail is email infrastructure — full IMAP/SMTP mailboxes, your domains, sending
				API and webmail on a server you own. It replaces the Exchange Online mailboxes in
				Microsoft 365, not the wider suite: Word, Excel and PowerPoint; Teams; SharePoint;
				OneDrive. Teams that also need those run Vectis Mail for the mailboxes and keep a
				separate productivity suite alongside it, so the per-seat <em>email</em> licence stops
				scaling with headcount. That's the scope — deliberately email, done properly, and owned
				outright.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Migrating email off Microsoft 365</h2>
		<p>
			Because Exchange Online speaks standard IMAP, moving mailboxes to Vectis Mail is mechanical
			rather than proprietary. The typical sequence:
		</p>
		<ol class="alt-steps">
			<li><strong>Stand up Vectis Mail</strong> on a fresh VPS following the <a href="/getting-started/installation/">installation guide</a>. Add your domain(s) and publish the DKIM/SPF/DMARC records it generates — but leave MX pointed at Microsoft for now.</li>
			<li><strong>Create the mailboxes</strong> in Vectis Mail to match your existing users (or import them via the API for larger lists).</li>
			<li><strong>Sync mail with <code>imapsync</code></strong>: copy each Exchange Online mailbox into Vectis Mail over IMAP. Run an initial pass days ahead, then a final delta sync at cutover.</li>
			<li><strong>Export calendar / contacts</strong> if you use them — Vectis Mail doesn't host calendaring yet (Phase 4 roadmap), so keep that on a suite or another tool. This is the step to plan around.</li>
			<li><strong>Warm your sending IP</strong> if you send volume. A fresh VPS IP has no reputation; follow the <a href="/deliverability/">dual-send warmup playbook</a> so there's no day-one deliverability gap.</li>
			<li><strong>Cut MX over</strong> to the Vectis Mail server once mailboxes are synced. Mail flows through Vectis Mail as DNS propagates.</li>
			<li><strong>Verify and decommission</strong>: watch logs for a few days, then drop the Exchange Online email licences you no longer need.</li>
		</ol>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>A note on deliverability</h2>
		<p>
			Microsoft delivers your mail from warm, well-reputed IP pools, so you never think about it.
			On a fresh self-hosted VPS, your IP has no reputation on day one and major providers will
			throttle until you establish a pattern over 2–4 weeks. This is real, and we don't hide it.
		</p>
		<p>
			The good news: you never switch cold. The <a href="/deliverability/">dual-send warmup
			playbook</a> keeps your current provider delivering while your own IP warms, so there's no
			day-one gap — just an overlap window you control. Once warm, a self-hosted IP with correct
			DKIM, SPF, DMARC, MTA-STS, and PTR records (all configured by default in Vectis Mail) can
			reach the major inboxes — at that point deliverability comes down to your IP reputation and
			DNS hygiene, not the platform you send from. Read the full honest answer on
			<a href="/deliverability/">Will my mail land?</a>
		</p>
	</section>

	<section class="section" style="padding-top: 1rem;">
		<h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
		<div class="alt-faq">
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Is there a self-hosted alternative to Microsoft 365 for email?</h3>
				<p>
					Yes. Vectis Mail is a self-hosted email server you run on your own VPS — Postfix,
					Dovecot, and Rspamd packaged with a REST API, a React admin UI, and an atomic update
					orchestrator. It replaces the email half of Microsoft 365 (the Exchange Online
					mailboxes): full IMAP/SMTP mailboxes, your own domains, DKIM/SPF/DMARC, and
					<a href="/pricing/">Roundcube webmail</a> for a flat price instead of per-user-per-month
					licensing. It does not replace Office apps or Teams — that's a separate need.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Does Vectis Mail replace Office apps and Teams?</h3>
				<p>
					No — Vectis Mail is email infrastructure. It replaces Exchange Online, the mailbox
					and mail-delivery half of Microsoft 365, not Word, Excel, PowerPoint, Teams, SharePoint,
					or OneDrive. If your organisation also needs those, run Vectis Mail for the mailboxes
					and keep a separate productivity suite alongside it. Our scope is full IMAP/SMTP
					mailboxes on your own server, flat-priced.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>How does Vectis Mail pricing compare to Microsoft 365 per-user licensing?</h3>
				<p>
					Microsoft 365 and Exchange Online are priced per user per month. Exchange Online Plan 1
					is about $4/user/month and Plan 2 about $8/user/month on annual commitment; Microsoft 365
					Business Standard rises to $14/user/month from 1 July 2026. That bill scales with
					headcount. Vectis Mail is flat: Starter is $0 self-hosted, Pro is $39 USD per tenant per
					month for unlimited mailboxes and domains. At 50 mailboxes, Business Standard runs about
					$700/month while Vectis Mail Pro plus a VPS is roughly $59/month — and the gap widens with
					every seat. See <a href="/pricing/">pricing</a>.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Can I keep my domain and use standard email clients?</h3>
				<p>
					Yes. Vectis Mail uses standard IMAP, SMTP, and POP3 with your own domain, so Outlook,
					Apple Mail, Thunderbird, and mobile clients connect the way they do to any standard mail
					server, plus a bundled Roundcube webmail. Because it's standard protocols on your domain,
					there's no Microsoft tenant to lock you in — migrating in or out is plain IMAP sync, not a
					proprietary export.
				</p>
			</div>
			<div class="alt-faq-q">
				<h3>Where does my email live with Vectis Mail versus Microsoft 365?</h3>
				<p>
					With Microsoft 365 your mailboxes live inside Microsoft's tenant, under their jurisdiction
					and subject to tenant-level outages. With Vectis Mail they live on a server you control, in
					the region and provider you choose. The message store is on your filesystem; the audit
					trail is in your own Postgres. For data-residency and sovereignty questions, the answer
					becomes "where did you deploy the server" instead of "trust the vendor."
				</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	<div class="cta-section">
		<h2>Try Vectis Mail</h2>
		<p>Own your mailboxes. Flat price, your server, standard protocols.</p>
		<div class="hero-buttons">
			<a href="/getting-started/installation/" class="btn-primary">Install Vectis Mail</a>
			<a href="/pricing/" class="btn-secondary">View Pricing</a>
		</div>
	</div>

	<section class="section other-alts">
		<h2>Other Vectis Mail alternatives</h2>
		<p class="subtitle">Comparing the big cloud suites and email APIs? Read the one that matches.</p>
		<div class="feature-grid" style="grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(280px, 1fr));">
			<a href="/alternatives/google-workspace/" class="feature-card alt-index-card">
